@NODE INTRO "Introduction"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}What is EmojiGear?@{ub}

EmojiGear is a Unicode-aware plain-text editor for AmigaOS 3.2.x (68k).
It can open, edit, and save text files encoded as UTF-8, ISO Latin-1,
(That is your legacy Amiga text format of all times.)
or ISO Latin-2, and renders all characters -- including colour Emoji --
using a built-in FreeType 2 engine.

@{b}Note on compatibility:@{ub} EmojiGear requires AmigaOS 3.2 or later.
Shared components may work on older systems.
@{" See OS 3.9 Compatibility Notes " LINK OS39COMPAT}

Unlike classic Amiga text editors that are limited to the system font
and an 8-bit codepage, EmojiGear:

  @{b}*@{ub}  Renders any TrueType or OpenType font (.ttf / .otf).
  @{b}*@{ub}  Displays full-colour Emoji from a CBDT/CBLC colour bitmap
     font such as NotoColorEmoji.ttf.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Supports up to three fallback fonts so exotic scripts are
     handled gracefully when the primary font lacks glyphs.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Opens multiple files at once, each in its own tab.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Interprets ANSI escape sequences for coloured terminal output.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Converts between encodings on load and save.

EmojiGear is built on two reusable Amiga shared components that any
application can use independently:

  @{b}utf8rastport.library@{ub}
    A standard AmigaOS shared library that exposes FreeType 2 rendering
    to any program.  It handles UTF-8 text layout, glyph rasterisation,
    emoji decoding and RastPort blitting.

  @{b}unitexteditor.gadget@{ub}
    A BOOPSI gadget class providing the full Unicode text editor widget.
    Any BOOPSI-aware application can embed it in its own windows.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE GETSTART "Getting Started"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Launching EmojiGear@{ub}

@{u}From the Shell:@{uu}

  To open an empty editor:

    1.Shell> EmojiGear

  To open one file:

    1.Shell> EmojiGear PROGDIR:mytext.txt

  To open several files (each in its own tab):

    1.Shell> EmojiGear file1.txt file2.txt file3.txt

  To open a file saved in an 8-bit encoding:

    1.Shell> EmojiGear oldtext.txt Latin1
    1.Shell> EmojiGear oldtext.txt Latin2

  The encoding keyword (Latin1 / Latin2, case-insensitive) applies to
  all file arguments and may appear anywhere in the argument list.

@{u}From Workbench:@{uu}

  Double-click the EmojiGear icon.  The program opens with a single
  blank tab.

  You can also drop a file icon onto the EmojiGear icon; the file will
  be opened automatically.

  See @{" Workbench / Tooltypes " LINK WORKBENCH} for more options.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE EDITOR "The Editor"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}The Editor Area@{ub}

The large area in the middle of the window is the text editor.  It
works like any standard text editor:

  @{b}*@{ub}  Click to position the cursor.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Click and drag, or Shift-click, to select text.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Type to insert characters at the cursor.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Backspace / Delete to erase characters.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Vertical and horizontal scroll bars are provided.

@{u}Status Bar@{uu}

A status bar at the bottom of the window shows:

  @{b}*@{ub}  @{b}Ready@{ub} when the file has not been modified since the last save.
  @{b}*@{ub}  @{b}Modified@{ub} when unsaved changes are present.
  @{b}*@{ub}  The current line and column number.
  @{b}*@{ub}  The file path of the active document.


@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE TABS "Working with Multiple Documents"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Tabs@{ub}

EmojiGear supports up to 20 open documents simultaneously.  Each
document occupies a tab in the tab bar near the top of the window.

@{u}Opening a new empty tab:@{uu}

  Select @{b}Project > New@{ub} from the menu.

@{u}Opening a file in a new tab:@{uu}

  Use any @{b}Project > Load@{ub} menu item.  The file is always opened in a
  new tab (or the tab is brought to front if the file is already open).

@{u}Switching between tabs:@{uu}

  @{b}*@{ub}  Click the tab with the mouse.
  @{b}*@{ub}  @{b}Ctrl+Tab@{ub}         -- next tab.
  @{b}*@{ub}  @{b}Ctrl+Shift+Tab@{ub}   -- previous tab.
  @{b}*@{ub}  @{b}Navigate > Next Text@{ub} and @{b}Navigate > Previous Text@{ub} in the menu.

@{u}Closing a tab:@{uu}

  @{b}*@{ub}  Click the window close gadget (top-left corner), or
  @{b}*@{ub}  Select @{b}Project > Close@{ub}.

  If it is the last remaining tab, closing it quits EmojiGear.

@{u}Tab labels:@{uu}

  A tab shows the file name portion of the path.  A new unsaved
  document is shown as @{b}New File@{ub}.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE MENUS "Menu Reference"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Menu Reference@{ub}

@{u}Project@{uu}

  @{b}New@{ub}
    Opens a blank document in a new tab.

  @{b}Load UTF-8 Text...@{ub}               (Amiga-O)
    Opens a file requester and loads the chosen file as UTF-8.

  @{b}Load ISO Latin-1 Text...@{ub}
    As above, but decodes the file as ISO 8859-1 (Western European),
    Which is the official text encoding on Amiga OS3.

  @{b}Load ISO Latin-2 Text...@{ub}
    As above, but decodes the file as ISO 8859-2 (Central European).
    Which is the official text encoding on Amiga OS3, for these countries,
    if configured like this.
     
  @{b}Open Recent@{ub}
    Sub-menu listing the last 8 files opened.  Shows filename and
    encoding.  Only visible when at least one file has been opened.

  @{b}Close@{ub}                             (Amiga-Q)
    Closes the current tab (or quits if it is the only tab).

  @{b}Save As UTF-8 Text...@{ub}             (Amiga-S)
    Saves the current document as a UTF-8 file.

  @{b}Save UTF-8 As Escaped ASCII...@{ub}
    Saves as plain ASCII; all non-ASCII characters are replaced by
    \uXXXX or \UXXXXXXXX escape sequences.

  @{b}Save As ISO Latin-1 Text...@{ub}
    Saves with ISO 8859-1 encoding.  Characters that cannot be
    represented are encoded with slach-x syntax.

  @{b}Save As ISO Latin-2 Text...@{ub}
    As above, for ISO 8859-2, You may need this for east european countries.

  @{b}About EmojiGear...@{ub}
    Shows the About page in a dedicated tab.

  @{b}Quit@{ub}
    Exits EmojiGear.

@{u}Edit@{uu}

  @{b}Cut@{ub}                               (Amiga-X)
    Cuts the selected text to the clipboard.

  @{b}Copy UTF-8@{ub}                        (Amiga-C)
    Copies the selection to the clipboard as UTF-8.

  @{b}Copy As ISO Latin-1@{ub}
    Copies, converting to Latin-1 when characters allow, else slach-x syntax.
    Latin-1 is the Amiga default text format, so use this if you may paste 
    in other Amiga text editor.

  @{b}Copy As ISO Latin-2@{ub}
    Copies, converting to Latin-2 (east europe).

  @{b}Paste UTF-8@{ub}                       (Amiga-V)
    Pastes UTF-8 text from the clipboard.

  @{b}Paste from ISO Latin-1@{ub}
    Pastes and converts from Latin-1.

  @{b}Paste from ISO Latin-2@{ub}
    Pastes and converts from Latin-2.

  @{b}Undo@{ub}                              (Amiga-Z)
    Undoes the last edit operation.

  @{b}Redo@{ub}                              (Amiga-Y)
    Redoes an undone operation.

  @{b}Emoji Box...@{ub}                      (Amiga-E)
    Opens the Emoji Box window for point-and-click emoji insertion.
    See @{" Emoji Box " LINK EMOJIBOX}.

@{u}Navigate@{uu}

  @{b}Find...@{ub}                           (Amiga-F)
    Opens the Find & Replace bar.

  @{b}Find Next@{ub}
    Jumps to the next occurrence of the current search term.

  @{b}Find Previous@{ub}
    Jumps to the previous occurrence.

  @{b}Go to Line...@{ub}                     (Amiga-G)
    Prompts for a line number and moves the cursor there.

  @{b}Previous Text@{ub}                     (Ctrl+Shift+Tab)
    Switches to the previous tab.

  @{b}Next Text@{ub}                         (Ctrl+Tab)
    Switches to the next tab.

@{u}Settings@{uu}

  @{b}Settings...@{ub}                       (Amiga-P)
    Opens the Settings window.  See @{" Settings " LINK SETTINGS}.

  @{b}Font Settings...@{ub}                  (Amiga-T)
    Opens the Font Settings window.  See @{" Font Settings " LINK FONTSETTINGS}.

  @{b}Word Wrap@{ub}          (toggle, on by default)
    Enables or disables automatic line wrapping at the window edge.

  @{b}Force Monospace@{ub}    (toggle)
    Renders all glyphs at the same fixed width regardless of the font.

  @{b}ANSI Escape Rendering@{ub}  (toggle)
    Interprets ANSI/VT100 colour and attribute escape sequences in
    the text (useful for log files and terminal captures).

  @{b}Use ANSI Unix Colors@{ub}   (toggle)
    When ANSI rendering is on, resolves colour codes through the
    xterm 256-colour palette and the screen's closest pen.

  @{b}Font Size +@{ub}                       (Ctrl++)
    Increases the display font size one step.

  @{b}Font Size -@{ub}                       (Ctrl+-)
    Decreases the display font size one step.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE KEYBOARD "Keyboard Shortcuts"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Keyboard Shortcuts@{ub}

  @{u}File operations@{uu}

    Amiga-O       Load UTF-8 file
    Amiga-S       Save as UTF-8
    Amiga-Q       Close current tab
    Amiga-P       Open Settings window
    Amiga-T       Open Font Settings window

  @{u}Editing@{uu}

    Amiga-Z       Undo
    Amiga-Y       Redo
    Amiga-X       Cut
    Amiga-C       Copy (UTF-8)
    Amiga-V       Paste (UTF-8)
    Amiga-E       Open Emoji Box

  @{u}Navigation@{uu}

    Amiga-F       Open Find & Replace bar
    Amiga-G       Go to Line
    Ctrl+Tab      Next tab
    Ctrl+Shift+Tab  Previous tab

  @{u}Display@{uu}

    Ctrl++        Font size larger
    Ctrl+-        Font size smaller

  @{u}Emoji F-keys@{uu}

    F1 .. F10               Insert emoji (row 1 of active set)
    Shift+F1 .. Shift+F10   Insert emoji (row 2)
    Ctrl+F1  .. Ctrl+F10    Insert emoji (row 3)
    Ctrl+Shift+F1 .. F10    Insert emoji (row 4)

    See @{" Emoji Box " LINK EMOJIBOX} for details.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE EMOJIBOX "Emoji Box"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Emoji Box@{ub}

Open the Emoji Box with @{b}Edit > Emoji Box...@{ub} (Amiga-E).

The window shows a grid of 40 emoji arranged in 4 rows and 10 columns.
The column headers read @{b}F1@{ub} through @{b}F10@{ub}, and the row headers show
the modifier combination required:

  @{b}(none)@{ub}           F1 .. F10
  @{b}Shift@{ub}            Shift+F1 .. Shift+F10
  @{b}Ctrl@{ub}             Ctrl+F1  .. Ctrl+F10
  @{b}Sh+Ctrl@{ub}          Ctrl+Shift+F1 .. Ctrl+Shift+F10

Pressing the corresponding F-key combination while the editor has focus
inserts that emoji directly into the text at the cursor position.

A chooser gadget at the top of the Emoji Box window selects the active
emoji set.

@{b}Tip:@{ub} You do not need to keep the Emoji Box open to use the F-keys.
Once you have learned the key assignments, close the window and use the
keyboard directly.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE FINDREPLACE "Find and Replace"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Find and Replace@{ub}

Open the Find & Replace bar with @{b}Navigate > Find...@{ub} (Amiga-F).
A panel appears below the tab bar with the following controls:

  @{b}Search :@{ub}      Type the text to find.
  @{b}Replace :@{ub}     Type the replacement text.
  @{b}X@{ub}             Clear the search or replace field.
  @{b}Find Next@{ub}     Jump to the next match.
  @{b}Find Previous@{ub} Jump to the previous match.
  @{b}Replace@{ub}       Replace the current match and advance.
  @{b}Replace All@{ub}   Replace all matches in the document.
  @{b}Case sensitive@{ub} Toggle case-sensitive matching.
  @{b}Close@{ub}         Hide the Find & Replace bar.

You can also use @{b}Navigate > Find Next@{ub} and @{b}Navigate > Find Previous@{ub}
from the menu, or @{b}Navigate > Go to Line...@{ub} (Amiga-G) to jump to a
specific line number.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE SETTINGS "Settings"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Settings Window@{ub}  (Amiga-P)

The Settings window lets you configure the editor appearance and
behaviour.  Changes take effect immediately and are saved to the
program's icon tooltypes when you close the window.

@{u}Editor display@{uu}

  @{b}Background Color@{ub}
    The background colour of the editor area.  Click the colour
    gadget to choose a new colour from the screen palette.

  @{b}Pen Color@{ub}
    The default text colour.

  @{b}Tab Spaces@{ub}
    Number of spaces that one tab character represents (2 to 12).

  @{b}Visualize Tabs@{ub}
    When checked, tab characters are drawn with a visible marker.

  @{b}Tab key does spaces@{ub}
    When checked, pressing Tab inserts spaces instead of a tab
    character.

@{u}Quick toggles (also in Settings menu)@{uu}

  @{b}Word Wrap@{ub}          Lines are wrapped at the window boundary.
  @{b}Force Monospace@{ub}    Fixed-width rendering for all glyphs.
  @{b}ANSI Escape Rendering@{ub}  Colour/style escape sequences rendered.
  @{b}Use ANSI Unix Colors@{ub}   xterm 256-colour palette for ANSI.

All settings are stored in the @{b}Tooltypes@{ub} of the EmojiGear icon.
On disk this means the @{b}EmojiGear.info@{ub} file -- the standard Amiga
icon file that sits alongside the EmojiGear executable.  Settings are
loaded automatically from it on every launch.

See also @{" Screen Mode " LINK SETTINGS} in the Settings window to choose
whether to open on the Workbench screen or a custom screen.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE FONTSETTINGS "Font Settings"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Font Settings Window@{ub}  (Amiga-T)

EmojiGear uses FreeType 2 to render TrueType and OpenType fonts.
Use the Font Settings window to choose which font files to use.

@{u}Fonts@{uu}

  @{b}Primary Font:@{ub}
    The main font for all text.  Supports .ttf and .otf files.
    Click the file gadget button (...) to browse with an ASL requester.

  @{b}Fallback Font 1:@{ub}
    Used for glyphs not found in the primary font.

  @{b}Fallback Font 2:@{ub}
    Second fallback, for even wider Unicode coverage.

  @{b}Emoji Font:@{ub}
    A colour emoji font (CBDT/CBLC, e.g. NotoColorEmoji.ttf).
    When a character is in an emoji range and this font is set,
    EmojiGear renders the full-colour bitmap version.

@{u}Options@{uu}

  @{b}Antialias when possible@{ub}
    Enables grey-level anti-aliasing.  May look sharper on hi-res
    screens; on lo-res or low-colour screens plain bitmaps look better.

  @{b}Emoji Quality scaling@{ub}
    When checked, emoji bitmaps are scaled with a higher-quality
    algorithm for smoother results at large sizes.

@{u}Presets@{uu}

  @{b}Low-end 2 colors@{ub}
    Switches to a configuration optimised for 2-colour screens
    (no antialiasing, monospace, small font size).

  @{b}High Quality@{ub}
    Enables antialiasing and proportional rendering.

  @{b}High Monospace@{ub}
    High quality but with fixed-width rendering (useful for code).

Font settings are saved in the icon tooltypes and restored on launch.

Note you can install yourself any ttf or otf font, and that each font
manages more or less characters: some arial.ttf have many alphabets.
There is a katakana alphabet in the Emoji Box sets, but these glyphs
are not available in any the installed fonts. unavailable characters
are drawn with a simple rectangle. You may download a font such as 
NotoSansJP-Regular.otf and add it to the font list, to enable 
katakana and japanese characters.
 Be warned that fonts are copyrighted artworks.


@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE FORMATS "File Formats and Encodings"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}File Formats and Encodings@{ub}

EmojiGear works internally in UTF-8 and converts on load and save.

@{u}UTF-8@{uu}

  The native encoding.  All Unicode characters (U+0000 to U+10FFFF)
  are supported.  Files may start with an optional UTF-8 BOM
  (EF BB BF), which is stripped on load.

  UTF-16 files (BE or LE, detected by BOM) are converted to UTF-8
  automatically on load.

@{u}ISO Latin-1  (ISO 8859-1)@{uu}

  Western European: French, German, Spanish, Italian, English, etc.
  Byte values 0xA0-0xFF map directly to U+00A0-U+00FF.
  Use @{b}Load ISO Latin-1@{ub} or pass @{b}Latin1@{ub} on the Shell command line.
  This is the Amiga OS3 encoding since the age of times, most OSes in
  the 90's would also use that.

@{u}ISO Latin-2  (ISO 8859-2)@{uu}

  Central/Eastern European: Czech, Polish, Slovak, Slovenian,
  Croatian, etc.
  Use @{b}Load ISO Latin-2@{ub} or pass @{b}Latin2@{ub} on the Shell command line.

@{u}Auto-detection@{uu}

  When loading with @{b}Load UTF-8 Text@{ub}, EmojiGear validates the bytes.
  If the file is not valid UTF-8 it is silently re-interpreted as
  Latin-1 so no data is lost.

@{u}Save as Escaped ASCII@{uu}

  Saves as 7-bit ASCII.  Every non-ASCII character is written as a
  \uXXXX (BMP) or \UXXXXXXXX (supplementary plane) escape.  This
  produces files that any ASCII-only editor can read while preserving
  all Unicode information.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE PIPEINPUT "Pipe Input from Shell Commands"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Piping Command Output into EmojiGear@{ub}

OS3.2 AmigaDOS supports the pipe operator @{b}|@{ub}, which feeds the 
standard output of one program directly into the standard input of another.
EmojiGear reads its standard input and inserts whatever it receives
into the current document.

This means you can use EmojiGear as a rich viewer for the output of
any shell command -- with full Unicode, Emoji and ANSI colour support
that the Amiga CLI window cannot display.

Note: since r4 if the character stream is not valid UTF8, it will be 
 treated as ANSI Latin1, so you may feed with unicode or legacy apps.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@{u}Basic usage@{uu}

  1.Shell> @{b}some-command | EmojiGear@{ub}

EmojiGear opens normally and the command's output streams into the
editor as it arrives, character by character.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@{u}Example: live weather report with colours@{uu}

  1.Shell> @{b}wget -O - http://wttr.in | EmojiGear@{ub}

@{b}wttr.in@{ub} is a console-oriented weather service that responds with
a fully formatted, ANSI-coloured, emoji-decorated weather report --
the kind of output that looks like a jumble of escape codes in the
standard Amiga CLI.

In EmojiGear, with the right display options set, the same output
is rendered exactly as a Unix shell would show it:

  @{b}*@{ub}  Temperature colours, cloud icons, wind arrows -- all in colour.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Weather emoji rendered at full size from the emoji font.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Monospace columns aligned exactly as intended.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@{u}Setting up for the best result@{uu}

Before piping ANSI-coloured output, enable these two options in the
@{b}Settings@{ub} menu (or in the Settings window, Amiga-P):

  @{b}1.  Force Monospace@{ub}
         Makes every character occupy the same width, so tables and
         columns line up correctly -- just as in a Unix terminal.
         Toggle: @{b}Settings > Force Monospace@{ub}.

  @{b}2.  ANSI Escape Rendering@{ub}
         Tells EmojiGear to interpret VT100/ANSI colour and style codes
         embedded in the text instead of displaying them as raw escape
         characters.  Without this option the output looks like:
           ESC[1;32mHello ESC[0m
         With it, "Hello" is displayed in bold green.
         Toggle: @{b}Settings > ANSI Escape Rendering@{ub}.

  @{b}Optional: Use ANSI Unix Colors@{ub}
         When also enabled, colour codes are resolved through the xterm
         256-colour palette, giving the most accurate colour mapping on
         RTG screens.  On a standard Workbench screen the basic 8-colour
         ANSI mode is sufficient.
         Toggle: @{b}Settings > Use ANSI Unix Colors@{ub}.

Both settings are remembered between sessions (stored in tooltypes).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@{u}What you get that the Amiga CLI cannot show@{uu}

The AmigaDOS CLI window uses the system console (CON:), which supports
only plain 7-bit ASCII and the Topaz bitmap font.  It cannot:

  @{b}*@{ub}  Render UTF-8 or Unicode characters.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Display colour emoji.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Interpret ANSI/VT100 colour escape sequences.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Use proportional or TrueType fonts.

EmojiGear with pipe input replaces the CLI window as a rich output
viewer, bringing the visual quality of a modern Unix terminal to
AmigaOS -- including colour weather maps, syntax-highlighted output
from Unix tools, and emoji-decorated reports.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@{u}More examples@{uu}

  Display the output of any tool that produces ANSI/UTF-8 text:

    1.Shell> @{b}wget -O - http://wttr.in/London | EmojiGear@{ub}
    1.Shell> @{b}wget -O - "http://rate.sx/" | EmojiGear@{ub}

  View a local UTF-8 text file with ANSI colours already embedded:

    1.Shell> @{b}type mylog.txt | EmojiGear@{ub}

  The output streams in as it arrives; EmojiGear does not need to wait
  for the command to finish before displaying the first lines.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE WORKBENCH "Running from Workbench / Tooltypes"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Running from Workbench@{ub}

Double-click the EmojiGear icon to launch the program.  A file icon
dropped onto the EmojiGear icon will be opened on startup.

EmojiGear can also be iconified: click the iconify gadget (the small
square in the top-right of the window title bar).  The program shrinks
to an AppIcon on the Workbench backdrop.  Double-clicking the AppIcon
brings the window back.

@{u}Tooltypes@{uu}

All persistent settings are stored in the icon tooltypes of the
EmojiGear icon.  You can inspect and change them from the Workbench
with @{b}Icons > Information...@{ub}.  Known tooltypes include:

  @{b}PRIMARYFONT=path@{ub}       Path to primary TrueType font.
  @{b}FALLBACK1=path@{ub}         Path to first fallback font.
  @{b}FALLBACK2=path@{ub}         Path to second fallback font.
  @{b}EMOJIFONT=path@{ub}         Path to colour emoji font.
  @{b}FONTSIZE=n@{ub}             Font size index (0=8pt, 1=12pt, ...).
  @{b}WORDWRAP=YES/NO@{ub}        Word wrap on or off.
  @{b}MONOSPACE=YES/NO@{ub}       Force monospace rendering.
  @{b}ANTIALIAS=YES/NO@{ub}       Antialiased rendering.
  @{b}TABSPACES=n@{ub}            Tab stop width in spaces.
  @{b}TABSARESPACES=YES/NO@{ub}   Tab key inserts spaces.
  @{b}VIZTABS=YES/NO@{ub}         Visualize tab characters.
  @{b}APPLYANSI=YES/NO@{ub}       ANSI escape rendering.
  @{b}RECENTFILE0=path@{ub}       Most recently opened file.
  @{b}RECENTFILE1=path@{ub}       ... (up to RECENTFILE7)

It is recommended to let EmojiGear manage these values through its
Settings and Font Settings windows rather than editing them by hand.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE REQUIRE "System Requirements"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}System Requirements@{ub}

  @{b}CPU:@{ub}          Motorola 68020 or better.
  @{b}OS:@{ub}           @{b}AmigaOS 3.2 or later (v47+).@{ub}
                 EmojiGear will @{b}not@{ub} run on AmigaOS 3.9 or earlier.
                 It uses several BOOPSI classes introduced in OS 3.2
                 (gadgets/clicktab.gadget v47, window.class v47, etc.)
                 that are not present on older systems.
  @{b}Chip RAM:@{ub}     At least 1 MB recommended.
  @{b}Fast RAM:@{ub}     At least 4 MB recommended.
                 More RAM allows larger files and more tabs.
  @{b}Libraries:@{ub}    All required shared libraries and BOOPSI
                 gadget classes are included with the program:

                   utf8rastport.library
                   gadgets/unitexteditor.gadget
                   gadgets/unibutton.gadget
                   gadgets/clicktab.gadget     (requires OS 3.2)
                   gadgets/layout.gadget
                   gadgets/button.gadget
                   gadgets/checkbox.gadget
                   gadgets/getfile.gadget
                   gadgets/integer.gadget
                   gadgets/palette.gadget
                   gadgets/chooser.gadget
                   window.class               (requires OS 3.2)
                   images/label.image
                   requester.class
                   gadgets/scroller.gadget

  @{b}Note for developers:@{ub}
                 @{b}utf8rastport.library@{ub} and @{b}unibutton.gadget@{ub} are
                 intentionally built for OS 3.9 compatibility so they
                 can be used as standalone SDK components in applications
                 that target older systems.  Only the EmojiGear
                 application itself is OS 3.2-only.

  @{b}Fonts:@{ub}        EmojiGear requires at least one TrueType or
                 OpenType font file (.ttf / .otf) for text rendering.
                 A colour emoji font (e.g. NotoColorEmoji.ttf) is
                 optional but recommended for full emoji display.

  @{b}Screens:@{ub}      Works on the Workbench screen or a custom AGA/
                 RTG screen.  RTG screens with 24-bit colour give the
                 best results for antialiased text and colour emoji.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE OS39COMPAT "AmigaOS 3.9 Compatibility"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}AmigaOS 3.9 Compatibility Notes@{ub}

@{u}EmojiGear application and unitexteditor.gadget@{uu}

Full OS 3.9 compatibility for @{b}unitexteditor.gadget@{ub} cannot be achieved.
The gadget relies on offscreen bitmap tile rendering (a pool of chip-RAM
bitmaps drawn via a shared Layer/RastPort), and there is a long-standing
bug in @{b}layers.library@{ub} on OS 3.9 that prevents this pattern from working
safely.  The bug was fixed in AmigaOS 3.2.  As a consequence, using
@{b}unitexteditor.gadget@{ub} or the @{b}EmojiGear@{ub} application on OS 3.9 can lead
to display corruption, crashes or system instability.

@{u}utf8rastport.library and unibutton.gadget@{uu}

@{b}utf8rastport.library@{ub} and @{b}unibutton.gadget@{ub} do not use offscreen
Layer rendering and are expected to work correctly on AmigaOS 3.9 and
even AmigaOS 3.1.  They can be used independently by any application
targeting those older systems.

@{u}MUImojiGear on OS 3.9@{uu}

OS 3.9 users may try @{b}MUImojiGear@{ub}, the MUI-based variant included in
this package.  MUI manages its own rendering pipeline and avoids the
layers.library issue.  However, because it still pulls in
@{b}utf8rastport.library@{ub} and performs intensive Unicode rendering, it may
eventually cause system instability on OS 3.9 under heavy use.
Use it at your own risk and save your work frequently.

@{u}Upgrading from OS 3.9 to OS 3.2@{uu}

The best solution is to upgrade.  Hyperion Entertainment provides an
official @{b}AmigaOS 3.2@{ub} installer that can be applied to any existing
OS 3.9 installation.  OS 3.2 corrects a large number of bugs present in
OS 3.9 -- including the layers.library issue -- and is the recommended
base for all current Amiga software development.

Information and purchase: @{b}http://www.hyperion-entertainment.com@{ub}

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE MUIALT "MUImojiGear - The MUI Alternative"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}MUImojiGear -- The MUI Alternative@{ub}

MUImojiGear is a MUI-based variant of EmojiGear included in this
package.  It was created to reach closer to OS 3.9 compatibility:
MUI manages its own window and gadget rendering, working around the
incomplete or buggy BOOPSI classes found on AmigaOS 3.0, 3.1, 3.5
and 3.9 that prevent EmojiGear from running on those systems.

@{u}Remaining OS 3.9 limitation@{uu}

MUImojiGear is still affected by the @{b}layers.library@{ub} bug present in
OS 3.9 (see @{" OS 3.9 Notes " LINK OS39COMPAT}).  In practice the editor
may run and appear stable during normal use, but the bug can manifest
at quit time or under heavy rendering load, making the system unstable.
OS 3.9 users should save their work frequently and be aware of this
risk.  OS 3.2 users can of course run MUImojiGear without any of these
concerns.

@{u}Open source -- contributions welcome@{uu}

Both @{b}EmojiGear@{ub} and @{b}MUImojiGear@{ub} are open source and openly available
for further development.  If you are familiar with AmigaOS internals
and feel daring enough to tackle the OS 3.9 layers.library issue, your
contributions are very welcome.

  Source code: @{b}https://github.com/krabobmkd/EmojiGear@{ub}

@{u}Features present in MUImojiGear@{uu}

Most of what EmojiGear offers is available in MUImojiGear in the
same way:

  @{b}*@{ub}  UTF-8, ISO Latin-1 and ISO Latin-2 encoding support.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Full emoji rendering via the Emoji Box (F-key assignment).
  @{b}*@{ub}  Cut, Copy, Paste (UTF-8 and Latin encodings).
  @{b}*@{ub}  Undo and Redo.
  @{b}*@{ub}  Font settings and all rendering options (antialias, monospace,
     high-quality emoji scaling, ANSI escape rendering).
  @{b}*@{ub}  Pipe input from Shell commands.

@{u}Known differences and quirks@{uu}

  @{b}*@{ub}  @{b}Font size shortcuts:@{ub} Amiga++ and Amiga+- change the font size
     on the fly, as in EmojiGear.  The key combinations work the same
     way but MUI intercepts some modifier combinations differently
     depending on your MUI version and keyboard setup, so the exact
     feel may vary slightly.

  @{b}*@{ub}  @{b}Keyboard focus:@{ub} MUI routes keyboard input through its own
     focus system.  After interacting with a button, menu or requester
     you may need to click inside the text area again to restore
     keyboard focus to the editor.  This is normal MUI behaviour.

@{u}Settings via icon tooltypes@{uu}

Some options that have no dedicated UI in MUImojiGear can be set
directly in the tooltypes of the @{b}MUImojiGear@{ub} icon.  Open the icon
information window from Workbench (@{b}Icons > Information...@{ub}) and add
or edit the relevant tooltypes there.  They are read on every launch.

This is particularly useful for colours: MUImojiGear does not have a
colour-picker panel, but you can set the editor text and background
colours precisely by adding the following tooltypes:

  @{b}EDITORPENCOLOR=0xRRGGBB@{ub}    text (foreground) colour as 24-bit hex
  @{b}EDITORBGCOLOR=0xRRGGBB@{ub}     background colour as 24-bit hex

Example: white text on a dark blue background:

  EDITORPENCOLOR=0xEEEEEE
  EDITORBGCOLOR=0x002244

All other persistent settings (fonts, rendering options, word wrap,
etc.) are also stored as tooltypes and can be tweaked the same way.

@{u}Features not yet present in MUImojiGear@{uu}

  @{b}*@{ub}  @{b}Multi-file support:@{ub} only one document can be open at a time;
     there is no tab bar.
  @{b}*@{ub}  @{b}Find & Replace:@{ub} text search and replace are not available.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE
